# Documentation and Observability

## Rustdoc
- Every crate has crate-level docs; every public module has module docs (C-CRATE-DOC, M-MODULE-DOCS).
- Every public item is documented (`missing_docs` lint). First sentence fits on one line, ~15 words
  (M-FIRST-DOC-SENTENCE).
- Canonical sections as applicable (M-CANONICAL-DOCS): `# Examples`, `# Errors`, `# Panics`, `# Safety`.
- Examples compile, use `?` rather than `unwrap` (C-QUESTION-MARK).
- When an item implements reference behavior, link its contract ID (e.g. `Contract: hooks/filter-dispatch`);
  upstream file references belong in the contract entry, not in Rust docs.
- Docs describe the end state, not the design journey (M-NO-META-DESIGN-DOCUMENTATION).
  Design reasoning belongs in `docs/adr/NNNN-title.md`.

## Comments
- Comments state constraints the code cannot show (ordering requirements, WordPress quirks being preserved,
  safety invariants). No narration of what the next line does, no commented-out code.
- Magic values are named constants with a doc comment explaining their origin (M-DOCUMENTED-MAGIC).

## Logging and tracing
- Use `tracing` with structured fields and message templates (M-LOG-STRUCTURED). No `println!`, `eprintln!`
  or `dbg!` in production code (M-LOG-NOT-PRINT; lint-enforced).

```rust
// ❌ tracing::info!("hook {} ran in {}ms", name, ms);
tracing::debug!(hook = %name, priority, elapsed_ms = ms, "hook dispatched");
```

- Instrument boundary functions (HTTP handlers, bridge crossings, DB calls, cron jobs) with spans carrying
  `site_id`, `request_id`, and relevant contract IDs.
- Logging on hot paths must be cheap when disabled (M-LOG-OVERHEAD).

## Secrets never leave the process
Never log, trace, panic-message, or `Debug`-print: passwords and hashes, auth/session cookies, nonces,
salts/keys from configuration, application passwords, payment data, or personal data from content.
Types holding secrets implement `Debug` manually with redaction.
